"q-7b26998971ec","stem":"The azygos vein receives blood from which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Histology and Tissues","topicOriginal":"Histology and Tissues","subtopic":"Vein","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"posterior intercostal veins"},{"label":"B","text":"anterior intercostal veins"},{"label":"C","text":"left internal jugular vein"},{"label":"D","text":"anterior jugular vein"},{"label":"E","text":"lateral thoracic vein"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Histology and Tissues","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-44664c73fc20","stem":"The hemiazygos vein does NOT receive blood from which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Histology and Tissues","topicOriginal":"Histology and Tissues","subtopic":"Vein","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"left subcostal vein"},{"label":"B","text":"ascending lumbar veins"},{"label":"C","text":"inferior three posterior intercostal veins"},{"label":"D","text":"superior phrenic veins"},{"label":"E","text":"small mediastinal veins"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Histology and Tissues","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7476a5e12685","stem":"Which of the following may compress the esophagus?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic arch"},{"label":"B","text":"Right main bronchus"},{"label":"C","text":"Left vagus"},{"label":"D","text":"Right brachiocephalic vein"},{"label":"E","text":"Brachiocephalic trunk"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-423115a69e29","stem":"An aneurysm of the axillary artery within the axilla is most likely to compress which of the following neural structures?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Neuroanatomy","topicOriginal":"Neuroanatomy","subtopic":"Neural","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillary nerve"},{"label":"B","text":"Long thoracic nerve"},{"label":"C","text":"Lower trunk of the brachial plexus"},{"label":"D","text":"Medial cord of the brachial plexus"},{"label":"E","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Neuroanatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f15723d7765c","stem":"As a result of a fall on the outstretched hand, a 19-year old college freshman suffers a hyperextension injury to her wrist. She is evaluated at the emergency room and diagnosed with an anterior dislocation of a carpal bone. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be compressed by this injury?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior interosseous nerve"},{"label":"B","text":"Median nerve"},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Radial nerve"},{"label":"E","text":"Ulnar nerve"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f58c361db940","stem":"After falling on his laterally outstretched arm, a patient suffered a dislocation of the glenohumeral joint. Which of the following nerves is most likely to have been injured from this dislocation?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillary nerve"},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal scapular nerve"},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral pectoral nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Medial pectoral nerve"},{"label":"E","text":"Suprascapular nerve"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a46ca93b42f4","stem":"A 43 –year old man presents with complaints of difficulty raising his right arm above his head. He reports that he had an accident at home in which heavy object had fallen onto his shoulder. Physical examination reveals that when the patient presses his hand against a wall, the medial border of his right scapular protrudes from his back. Which of the following nerves was most likely injured?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillary nerve"},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal scapular nerve"},{"label":"C","text":"Long thoracic nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Suprascapular nerve"},{"label":"E","text":"Thoracodorsal nerve"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-62d109fbb7d8","stem":"A physician examining a neonate is startled to find that squeezing the infant’s shoulders with his hand can bring them nearly together in front of the body. Congenital absence of which of the following bones should be suspected?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clavicle"},{"label":"B","text":"First rib"},{"label":"C","text":"Humerus"},{"label":"D","text":"Scapulae"},{"label":"E","text":"Sternum"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4154b0561b9a","stem":"A fracture of the humerus neck damages a nerve running around the humeral neck. After the fracture heals, the patient notices a marked decrease in his ability to raise his arm over his head. The denervated muscle arises from which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acromion alone"},{"label":"B","text":"Clavicle and acromion"},{"label":"C","text":"Clavicle and coracoids"},{"label":"D","text":"Coracoid alone"},{"label":"E","text":"Coracoid and acromion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5ff498d9a3c","stem":"Stenosis of the bicuspid/mitral atrioventricular valve may initially cause an increase of pressure in the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"superior and inferior venae cavae"},{"label":"B","text":"left ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"pulmonary circuit"},{"label":"D","text":"coronary circuit"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1063256ceb40","stem":"An 80-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of a lump in her right breast. Physical examination shows a 2-cm mass in the right breast with dimpling of the overlying skin. Examination of a biopsy specimen confirms a diagnosis of carcinoma. Involvement of which of the following structures is the most likely cause of this patient's skin dimpling?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Areola"},{"label":"B","text":"Lactiferous ducts"},{"label":"C","text":"Retromammary bursa"},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial fascia"},{"label":"E","text":"Suspensory ligaments"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58797c85e9e6","stem":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician for a routine health maintenance examination. He has no history of major medical illnesses. Vital signs are within normal limits. During the examination, the physician wishes to listen for sounds from the aortic valve. For optimal results, it is most appropriate for the stethoscope to be placed along which of the following sternal borders of the anterior chest wall?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left lower"},{"label":"B","text":"Left upper"},{"label":"C","text":"Right lower"},{"label":"D","text":"Right upper"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-365fe3c429dd","stem":"A 40-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of the sensation that food gets lodged within her chest soon after she swallows. She has a history of rheumatic heart disease in childhood. Physical examination shows no supraclavicular adenopathy. A grade 2/6, apical diastolic murmur is heard that is described as a \"rumble.\" Enlargement of which of the following cardiovascular structures best explains this patient's dysphagia?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior vena cava"},{"label":"B","text":"Left atrium"},{"label":"C","text":"Left ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary trunk"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f412c1df14a9","stem":"A 75-year-old woman has congestive heart failure. An abnormality of which of the following valves is most likely causing the enlarged chamber indicated by the arrow on the lateral x-ray shown? [pic] | |","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral"},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary"},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspid"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d07ca1136d9","stem":"A 50-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 4-hour history of substernal chest pain with radiation to the neck and jaw. His pulse is 110/min and regular, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 140/90 mm Hg. The lungs are clear to auscultation. Coronary angiography shows obstruction of the right coronary artery. Which of the following portions of the heart is most likely ischemic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior two thirds of the interventricular septum"},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior wall of the left ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior (diaphragmatic) wall of the left ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral wall of the left ventricle"},{"label":"E","text":"Left atrium"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cebbf1db613b","stem":"Examination of a full-term male newborn shows muffled heart sounds on the left and the point of maximal impulse on the right. An x-ray of the chest shows a right-sided cardiac silhouette. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this finding?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal looping of the primitive heart tube"},{"label":"B","text":"Dextroposition of the truncus arteriosus"},{"label":"C","text":"Persistent bulbus cordis"},{"label":"D","text":"Persistent primitive pulmonary vein"},{"label":"E","text":"Persistent right horn of the sinus venosus"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3d2854c0f130","stem":"A full-term female newborn with Down syndrome has an enlarged liver, peripheral edema, and pulmonary crackles. An x-ray of the chest shows cardiomegaly involving all four chambers, and echocardiography shows free communication between all chambers of the heart. Which of the following is the developmental basis for this cardiac problem?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal development of the truncal ridges and aorticopulmonary septum"},{"label":"B","text":"Abnormal resorption of the septum primum during formation of the foramen secundum"},{"label":"C","text":"Failure of fusion of the superior and inferior endocardial cushions"},{"label":"D","text":"Failure of constriction of the muscular wall of the ductus arteriosus after birth"},{"label":"E","text":"Failure of development of the membranous portion of the interventricular septum"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3e086f9f8722","stem":"A 2-day-old male newborn dies of respiratory failure. He was born at 27 weeks' gestation and had an uncomplicated vaginal delivery. Lung tissue obtained at autopsy is shown. Which of the following best explains this finding? [pic]","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascending intrauterine infection"},{"label":"B","text":"Cytomegalovirus pneumonia"},{"label":"C","text":"Hyaline membrane disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Meconium aspiration"},{"label":"E","text":"Postnatal bacterial pneumonia | |"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4cfedb50c7a1","stem":"During replacement of a dental crown, a 20-year-old man suddenly begins to choke. He recovers, but the dentist requests an x- ray of the chest because part of the crown is missing. Based on the x-ray findings shown, in which of the following structures is the crown most likely to be found? [pic]","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophagus"},{"label":"B","text":"Right lower lobe bronchus"},{"label":"C","text":"Right main bronchus"},{"label":"D","text":"Right middle lobe bronchus"},{"label":"E","text":"Right upper lobe bronchus"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1f562ee536d2","stem":"A 25-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after sustaining a stab wound in the second right intercostal space at the midclavicular line. She is having difficulty breathing. Physical examination shows decreased breath sounds on the right side of the chest. A chest x-ray is most likely to show an abnormally increased amount of air in which of the following?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pericardial space"},{"label":"B","text":"Pleural space"},{"label":"C","text":"Atria of the heart"},{"label":"D","text":"Lungs"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2ba873902877","stem":"Which of the following represents the primary function of the cell identified by the arrow in the histologic section shown? [pic]","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electrical impulse generation"},{"label":"B","text":"Immunoglobulin synthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Maintenance of vascular tone"},{"label":"D","text":"Oxygen transport"},{"label":"E","text":"Pulmonary surfactant secretion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4a2c70b5611e","stem":"A 25-year-old man has the recent onset of a fever and productive cough. There is a sharp, stabbing pain in the midlateral area of the left side of the chest with inspiration and coughing. Acute pneumonia is diagnosed. Which of the following could be infiltrated with lidocaine to decrease the severity of the pain?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parasympathetic innervation of the bronchioles"},{"label":"B","text":"Parasympathetic innervation of the pericardial sac"},{"label":"C","text":"Somatic innervation of the central diaphragmatic pleura"},{"label":"D","text":"Somatic innervation of the mediastinal pleura"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b23b0d9d6c09","stem":"An investigator is studying the mechanism of pulmonary edema. He investigates the normal flow of extracellular fluid through the lungs. Which of the following best describes the normal sequential path of extracellular fluid in the lungs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alveolar capillary → alveolar lymph duct → pulmonary lymphatic channel"},{"label":"B","text":"Alveolar capillary → alveolar space → interstitial space → lymphatic channel"},{"label":"C","text":"Alveolar capillary → interstitial space → lymphatic channel"},{"label":"D","text":"Alveolar capillary → interstitial space → pulmonary venule"},{"label":"E","text":"Prealveolar capillary → interstitial space → postalveolar capillary"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7a5694a4f1b0","stem":"A 33-year-old man is admitted to the hospital because of fever and chest pain. Crepitant crackles are heard in the left posterior lung base. Posteroanterior x-rays of the chest show obliteration of the left hemidiaphragm by a water-density process; the left cardiac border is clearly visible. Which of the following is the most likely location of the pathologic process?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior segment of the left upper lobe"},{"label":"B","text":"Apicoposterior segment of the left upper lobe"},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior lingular segment of the left upper lobe"},{"label":"D","text":"Superior lingular segment of the left upper lobe"},{"label":"E","text":"Left lower lobe"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d83dbcde57e2","stem":"A 53-year-old man has a neoplasm in the hilus of the right lung, anterior to the primary bronchus. If the neoplasm continues to grow in an anterior direction, which of the following structures in the surrounding area is most likely to become compressed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azygos vein"},{"label":"B","text":"Esophagus"},{"label":"C","text":"Phrenic nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathetic chain"},{"label":"E","text":"Vagus nerve"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0c46049362ec","stem":"A 3-hour-old male newborn has respiratory distress. An x-ray shows abdominal contents in the thoracic cavity. Failure of embryologic development of which of the following is the most likely explanation for this finding?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Embryology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal mesentery"},{"label":"B","text":"Migration of neural ectoderm"},{"label":"C","text":"Pleuroperitoneal membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"Rotation of foregut"},{"label":"E","text":"Ventral mesentery"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Embryology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ac9697226b47","stem":"A 27-year-old woman, gravida 1, para 0, comes to the emergency department 24 hours after premature rupture of the membranes at 32 weeks' gestation. Twelve hours later she delivers a 1390-g (3-lb 1-oz) male newborn. The newborn develops sepsis and dies at 30 hours of age. At autopsy, microscopic examination of the lungs shows respiratory bronchioles ending in clusters of terminal sacs lined by types I and II pneumocytes. Which of the following best explains these findings?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A defect in pulmonary development due to fetal exposure to a teratogenic agent early in the second trimester"},{"label":"B","text":"A defect in pulmonary development, frequently seen in infants with intrauterine infections"},{"label":"C","text":"Delayed development of the fetal lung associated with intrauterine hypoxia"},{"label":"D","text":"A developmental defect in endodermal derivatives usually associated with aplasia of portions of the gastrointestinal tract"},{"label":"E","text":"Normal respiratory development for this gestational age"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7908764671c0","stem":"A newborn who is born 7 weeks prematurely develops rapid, labored breathing within 1 hour of birth. This condition is due to insufficient production of the secretory product of which of the following cells in the respiratory system?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clara cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Goblet cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary macrophages"},{"label":"D","text":"Type I pneumocytes"},{"label":"E","text":"Type II pneumocytes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b4b3a552b3db","stem":"A right-handed 21-year-old college student comes to the physician because of pain in his right shoulder that developed after starting a summer job on a construction crew 2 weeks ago. The pain worsens when he lifts construction materials. Physical examination shows pain on resisted abduction at the right shoulder. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infraspinatus tendinitis"},{"label":"B","text":"Gout"},{"label":"C","text":"Degenerative arthritis of the shoulder"},{"label":"D","text":"Acromioclavicular joint arthritis"},{"label":"E","text":"Supraspinatus tendinitis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7bf62a266e27","stem":"A 45-year-old man comes to the physician because of right elbow pain. He works as a gardener and has just completed the 3 busiest months of the year. The pain seems to be concentrated in his lateral elbow, and it increases when he shovels or rakes. Examination shows tenderness over the right lateral humeral epicondyle. Inflammation of which of the following structures most likely accounts for his symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulnar collateral ligament"},{"label":"B","text":"Common extensor tendon"},{"label":"C","text":"Common flexor tendon"},{"label":"D","text":"Radial collateral ligament"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-279b49c9cd86","stem":"After falling from his bicycle, a 30-year-old man has severe wrist pain that is localized between the extensor pollicis longus and the extensor pollicis brevis. This pain is best explained by a fracture of which of the following carpal bones?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hamate"},{"label":"B","text":"Scaphoid"},{"label":"C","text":"Lunate"},{"label":"D","text":"Trapezoid"},{"label":"E","text":"Pisiform"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bd3331cc1b98","stem":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after dislocating his shoulder while playing soccer. Following reduction of the dislocation, he has pain over the dorsal region of the shoulder and cannot initiate abduction. An MRI of the shoulder shows a torn muscle. Which of the following muscles is most likely to have been damaged by this injury?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pectoralis minor"},{"label":"B","text":"Coracobrachialis"},{"label":"C","text":"Supraspinatus"},{"label":"D","text":"Teres major"},{"label":"E","text":"Long head of the triceps"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cba912a69d92","stem":"Moving the forearm against resistance from palm-down to palm-up (supination) position requires the use of which of the following muscles?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor carpi radialis"},{"label":"B","text":"Biceps brachii"},{"label":"C","text":"Pronator teres"},{"label":"D","text":"Triceps"},{"label":"E","text":"Brachialis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-89ef59ddbdc8","stem":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after sustaining a deep laceration that may have injured the nerve supply to the flexor digitorum profundus muscle of the index finger. Which of the following movements can be used to specifically test for the function of this muscle?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexion at the distal interphalangeal joint"},{"label":"B","text":"Flexion at the metacarpophalangeal joint"},{"label":"C","text":"Flexion at the proximal interphalangeal joint"},{"label":"D","text":"Adduction of a finger"},{"label":"E","text":"Abduction of a finger"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e766e32231cc","stem":"A 22-year-old man has pain and moderate swelling over the dorsomedial aspect and in the hypothenar area of the right hand after striking a wall with his clenched fist. Which of the following is the most likely finding on an x-ray of the hand?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dislocation of the fifth metacarpophalangeal joint"},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture of the proximal phalanx of the little finger"},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture of the fifth metacarpal bone"},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of the proximal phalanx of the ring finger"},{"label":"E","text":"Fracture of the triquetral bone"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dc481ba6b73e","stem":"A 53-year-old woman undergoes surgical removal of the right breast after being diagnosed with breast carcinoma. Postoperatively, there is an area of numbness of the skin of the medial arm. No motor losses are noted. Which of the following nerves was most likely damaged during the operation?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Intercostobrachial"},{"label":"C","text":"Long thoracic"},{"label":"D","text":"Radial"},{"label":"E","text":"Supraclavicular"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b31e504d0a0d","stem":"A male newborn has his left upper extremity in adduction and internal rotation with the forearm pronated. The startle reflex is absent on the left side. He was delivered by forceps following a difficult birth. A direct injury to which of the following sites of the brachial plexus is most likely?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral cord"},{"label":"B","text":"Medial cord"},{"label":"C","text":"Roots of the lower trunk"},{"label":"D","text":"Root of the middle trunk"},{"label":"E","text":"Roots of the upper trunk"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8039b7dd6d8b","stem":"A 13-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department after an accident during a motorbike race in which he was run over by several of the other racers. He has many cuts and bruises. He is unable to extend the left wrist, fingers, and thumb. Sensation is lost in the lateral half of the dorsum of the left hand. Which of the following nerves is most likely injured?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Median nerve, anterior wrist"},{"label":"B","text":"Median nerve, arm"},{"label":"C","text":"Radial nerve, midhumerus"},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve, midlateral forearm"},{"label":"E","text":"Ulnar nerve, midpalmar region"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-084839249c31","stem":"A male newborn delivered at term has webbing between the ring and small fingers of the left hand. Which of the following mechanisms involving the interdigital space is the most likely cause of this finding?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased apoptosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Excess proliferation"},{"label":"C","text":"Failure of cells to migrate"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased deposition of extracellular matrix"},{"label":"E","text":"Migration of cells"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c899aca318f0","stem":"The ductus arteriosus sometimes remains open after birth, requiring surgical closure. When placing a clamp on the ductus, care must be taken to avoid injury to what important structure immediately dorsal to it?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accessory hemiazygos vein"},{"label":"B","text":"Left internal thoracic artery"},{"label":"C","text":"Left phrenic nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"},{"label":"E","text":"Thoracic duct"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-68af74d565cf","stem":"Because of scarring of a patient's median cubital vein, the technician chooses to insert an infusion needle into her basilic vein at the level of the medial epicondyle. Despite the certainty that the needle does not pass through the deep (investing) fascia, there is still a chance that it might nick or impale which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Histology and Tissues","topicOriginal":"Histology and Tissues","subtopic":"Vein","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brachial artery"},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve"},{"label":"C","text":"Medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Median nerve"},{"label":"E","text":"Radial nerve"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Histology and Tissues","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6f1bfed20792","stem":"A man suffers a penetrating wound through the anterior axillary fold, with resulting damage to one of the main terminal branches of the brachial plexus. Among the effects is a significant weakening of flexion of the elbow. One or more other effects to be expected is (are)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Brachial","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of cutaneous sensation on the tips of several fingers"},{"label":"B","text":"Only loss of cutaneous sensation on the anterolateral surface of the forearm"},{"label":"C","text":"Only weakening of flexion at the shoulder"},{"label":"D","text":"Weakening of flexion at the shoulder and loss of cutaneous sensation on the anterolateral surface of the arm"},{"label":"E","text":"Weakening of flexion at the shoulder and loss of cutaneous sensation on the anterolateral surface of the forearm"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9efb4a08c62f","stem":"Neurological testing of a patient reveals no cutaneous sensation on the tip of the index finger. Such a finding would indicate injury to some portion of which nerve?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Median"},{"label":"C","text":"Musculocutaneous"},{"label":"D","text":"Radial"},{"label":"E","text":"Ulnar"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aa437bf75b9c","stem":"You are attending an axillary lymph node dissection in a patient with a melanoma in the upper limb. The surgeon says, \"We are going to sample the level II lymph nodes posterior to the pectoralis minor muscle.\" Having excelled in anatomy, you realize that she is referring to the anatomical nodes known as","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Upper Limb","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"apical axillary nodes"},{"label":"B","text":"central axillary nodes"},{"label":"C","text":"lateral axillary nodes"},{"label":"D","text":"lateral pectoral nodes"},{"label":"E","text":"subscapular axillary nodes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-93e2b0d59062","stem":"An elderly man complained of pain in his shoulder when he brought his forearm and hand behind his back while dressing. It was determined that the pain was caused by stretching of the lateral rotators of his arm during this motion. Which muscle was most likely involved?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infraspinatus"},{"label":"B","text":"Latissimus dorsi"},{"label":"C","text":"Subscapularis"},{"label":"D","text":"Supraspinatus"},{"label":"E","text":"Teres major"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5186fcd2425a","stem":"Due to the bleeding, the surgeon ligated (tied off) the suprascapular artery as it crossed the top of the scapula. There was no concern however, as she knew that direct collateral branches of which artery would supply the normal distribution of the suprascapular artery.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Histology and Tissues","topicOriginal":"Histology and Tissues","subtopic":"Artery","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"anterior circumflex humeral"},{"label":"B","text":"posterior circumflex humeral"},{"label":"C","text":"scapular circumflex"},{"label":"D","text":"subscapular"},{"label":"E","text":"thoracodorsal"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Histology and Tissues","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5e93e5b4016e","stem":"The pulse of the radial artery at the wrist is felt immediately lateral to which tendon?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Wrist","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abductor pollicis longus"},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor pollicis longus"},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor carpi radialis"},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus"},{"label":"E","text":"Palmaris longus"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5bc0041afeea","stem":"After falling on the ice, it was determined that a patient had a Colles' fracture. Care must be taken to relieve tension on the broken distal end of the radius created by the pull of which muscle?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor carpi ulnaris"},{"label":"B","text":"Brachioradialis"},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor carpi radialis longus"},{"label":"D","text":"Pronator quadrates"},{"label":"E","text":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58ddeeda58cd","stem":"In a plain radiograph of the chest, rotation is assessed by measuring?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The distance of the clavicle from the pedicles."},{"label":"B","text":"The distance of the clavicular heads from the corresponding spinous process"},{"label":"C","text":"The distance of the clavicular heads from each other"},{"label":"D","text":"The curvature of the spin on a lateral film."}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2931bd0e51e3","stem":"Which option lists the three structural types of fibrous joints?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Joint classification","options":[{"label":"A","text":"plane/gliding, hinge , pivot, saddle, condyloid"},{"label":"B","text":"condyloid, ball and socket, symphyses"},{"label":"C","text":"sutures, synchondroses, hinge, condyloid, plane"},{"label":"D","text":"syndesmoses, gomphoses, sutures"},{"label":"E","text":"synchondroses, symphyses"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_checked","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"checked_explained","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":[{"field":"stem","original":"Which of the following are subtypes of fibrous joints?","corrected":"Which option lists the three structural types of fibrous joints?","reason":"language_or_captured_footer_repair"}]},{"id":"q-9e9dfe2c4dbf","stem":"What type of joints are metacarpal/metatarsal and proximal phalanges?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"condyloid joints"},{"label":"B","text":"saddle joints"},{"label":"C","text":"ball and socket joints"},{"label":"D","text":"planar joints"},{"label":"E","text":"pivot joint"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-12eeef4411ac","stem":"Regarding ultrasound of the upper limb, which one is NOT the correct answer?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Used to asses blood flow and its velocity"},{"label":"B","text":"Used to asses joint effusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Has low accuracy to asses rotator cuff tendons tears"},{"label":"D","text":"Has high accuracy in assessing abscess"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem","prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4d574981f32f","stem":"The radial styloid process articulate with which among the following carpal bones","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic"