"The type II, type III and type IV hypersensitivity reactions can be suppressed by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"B","text":"Antihistaminics"},{"label":"C","text":"Corticosteroids"},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium cromoglycate"},{"label":"E","text":"Ketoconazole SECTION B (20 MARKS) Comment on the space at the right side of the table whether the following statements are True or False. PLEASE use T for True statement and F for a False statement | |Statement |True or False | |1 |Acute and chronic therapy with opioids may cause diarrhoea by decreasing intestinal motility, | | | |which results in prolonged transit time and increased absorption of fecal water | | |2 |Loperamide is an opioid antagonist that does not cross the blood-brain barrier and has no | | | |analgesic properties or potential for addiction | | |3 |Tegaserod is a serotonin 5-HT 4 partial agonist that has high affinity for 5-HT 4 receptors but| | | |no appreciable binding to 5-HT 3 or dopamine receptor | | |4 |5-HT 3 -receptor antagonists are the primary agents for the prevention | | | |of acute chemotherapy-induced nausea and emesis. | | |5 |5-ASA drugs induce and maintain remission in ulcerative colitis and are considered to be the | | | |first-line agents for treatment of mild to moderate active ulcerative colitis. | | |6 |Sulfasalazine has a high incidence of adverse effects, most of which are attributable to | | | |systemic effects of the sulfapyridine molecule. | | |7 |Lead can induce an anemia that may be either normocytic or microcytic and hypochromic | | |8 | | | | | | | | | | | | |Unithiol and succimer increase urine mercury excretion following acute or chronic elemental | | | |mercury inhalation | | | | | | |9 |Median lethal dose is the smallest dose that is observed to kill any experimental animal | | |10 |Once the drug has been approved to market, drug enters into phase 3 of clinical trial which | | | |constitutes monitoring the safety of the new drug under actual conditions of use in large | | | |numbers of patients. | | SECTION C (10 MARKS) Matching Items.There are two Columns A and B with statements. You are required to match the correct answer from column B that corresponds to statement from Column A. | |Column A | |Column B | |1 |Its bioavailability is increased by Saquinavir |A |Can be used for treament of open angle glaucoma | |2 |Acts synergistically |B |Cyproheptadine | |3 |Therapeutic effects of drugs may be limited by |C |They cause mydriasis | | |the activation of a physiological control loop | | | |4 |It displaces digoxin from tissue binding sites |D |Ritonavir | | |leading to digoxin toxicity | | | |5 |It can be used for prophylaxis of migraine in |E |They are widely used and effective in treating eczema, | | |refractory cases | |lichen planus, discoid lupus erythematosus, lichen | | | | |simplex chronicus and palmar plantar pustulosis, | |6 |Benzoyl peroxide or retinoic acid (tretinoin) |F |Administration of antidotes –acetylcysteine or | | | | |methionine | |7 |Topical glucocorticosteroids |G |Combination of sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim | |8 |Muscarinic antagonists |H |Quinidine | | |(anticholinergics) and sympathomimetics | | | |9 |Acetazolamide |I |Examples include the combination of an angiotensin | | | | |converting enzyme inhibitor with a diuretic | |10 |Paracetamol |J |They are keratolytic (peeling) agents used for treatment| | | | |of acne | SECTION D (20 MARKS) Write short notes on the following:-"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4bb1ba7f4cfa","stem":"What is prescription? What are the parts of prescription? (5marks)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1a50b555a183","stem":"You work as a medical doctor in the Internal Medicine Department at Hubert Kairuki Memorial Hospital. Mr. Shikibingi is a 45 year old male who presents to your clinic with epigastric abdominal pain of 2 weeks. He describes it as a burning pain which is non-radiating and is worse after he eats. He has frequent belching with bloating sensation but denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, constipation, or weight loss. He has tried Maalox which do help a little. expect to find? condition?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology","topicOriginal":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology","subtopic":"Diarrhoea","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"What is your diagnosis? Which symptoms do this patient have that support your diagnosis?"},{"label":"B","text":"What laboratory test(s) and other medical investigation(s) will you require in order to confirm your diagnosis? What will"},{"label":"C","text":"What are the treatment goals you would like to achieve in this case?"},{"label":"D","text":"Based on their pharmacological classification, what are the FOUR groups of drugs that are can be used to treat his medical"},{"label":"E","text":"Based on your findings in part (b) and your treatment goals above, what are FOUR pharmacological options do you have for this case? (20marks)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4545bc35cc9","stem":"Mr. Kambwende, a 70-year-old was admitted at Hubert Kairuki Memorial Hospital with miliary tuberculosis. In the past he had had a mitral valve replaced, and he had been on warfarin ever since. Treatment was commenced with isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide, and the INR was closely monitored in anticipation of increased warfarin requirements. He was discharged after several weeks with the INR in the therapeutic range on a much increased dose of warfarin. Rifampicin was subsequently discontinued. Two weeks later the patient was again admitted, this time drowsy and complaining of headache after mildly bumping his head on a locker. His pupils were unequal","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"What laboratory test will you order and why?"},{"label":"B","text":"What will you expect to find in your investigation/lab results?"},{"label":"C","text":"Why do you think this patient is experiencing this drug related problem? Please explain in detail."},{"label":"D","text":"How will you manage this case? (20marks)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unveri