y describe the posterior compartment of the leg?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"Leg","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5ca0f1618a01c4b62df198fcc8f24ae2a3966603"},{"id":"q-dfcaa7d615a7","stem":"20 Which statement, regarding the arterial anastomosis around the hip joint is incorrect?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"Hip","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f2d471b4d73","stem":"21 Which statement best describes the common femoral artery?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"Femoral","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c6253be3138c4d7510ecc3edd30eb2907b303be3"},{"id":"q-adb6ec149ed3","stem":"22 Which of the following associations of lower limb move- ments and root values is not correct?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"Lower Limb","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b38a8824e19f7620552e3a4d307476da310cf17e"},{"id":"q-6cc3b27d403a","stem":"5 cm below the tendinous arch in soleus, and descends along the medial crest of the fibula between tibialis poster- ior and flexor hallucis longus. While it gives perforating branches which supply the lateral compartment structures, it remains in the posterior compartment. LOWER LIMB: ANSWERS 45","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"Lower Limb","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"Peroneus tertius is a muscle of the anterior compartment."},{"label":"D","text":"The peroneus brevis tendon lies anterior to the peroneus longus tendon throughout, and as such lies in contact with the lateral malleolus which it grooves. The tendon of per- oneus longus does not come into contact with the lateral malleolus."},{"label":"E","text":"They act to evert the foot and are also weak plantarflex- ors. They have a notable role in plantarflexion if the tib- ial nerve is damaged. Peroneus longus also contributes to maintaining the arches of the foot."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_norma