 drainage of the head and neck is incorrect?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Head","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-01b32e474c34","stem":"where it is superficial, easily palpable and prone to injury.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-78d9d66c604f","stem":"which is palpable on the postero-medial aspect of the knee.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"Knee","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1909747e73e2","stem":"which renders it liable to damage in a loin incision.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ca3a82759cdb","stem":"Attempt all questions. SECTION I - MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTION (@1mark) The Dura fold that divides the two cerebellar hemispheres is called? Which cell is a macrophage found in the central nervous system? Cerebrospinal fluid enters the subarachnoid space via which of the following? Which of the following is an element of the central nervous system? What are the most abundant neurons in the body? A cell body of a neuron is called? What provides tensile strength to a neuron? Which meninx is made of a delicate web like connective tissue? Congloxmerations of gray matter deep within the cerebrum and cerebellum are called? Which of the following is involved in the blood cerebrospinal fluid - brain barrier? Which of the following is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Falx cerebri"},{"label":"B","text":"Falx cerebelli"},{"label":"C","text":"Transverse sinus"},{"label":"D","text":"Tentorium cerebelli Regarding cells of the nervous system;"},{"label":"E","text":"Pyramidal neurons are pseudopolar"},{"label":"F","text":"Schimid - Lantermann clefts occurs in myelin"},{"label":"G","text":"Nodes of Ranvier facilitate salutatory conductions"},{"label":"H","text":"Oligodendrocytes myelinate nerve fibers in the CNS i) Microglia j) Histiocytes k) Kupffer cells l) Langerhans cell Wallerian degeneration refers to: m) Degeneration of axons and their myelin sheaths distal to the injury. n) Loss of the myelin sheath around an axon, as in Guillain-Barre syndrome. o) Death of the cell body after axonal injury p) Neuronal degeneration in the CNS as opposed to the PNS. Regeneration of a peripheral nerve can occur best if: q) Peripheral nerve regeneration with functional recovery never can occur r) Re-anastomosis is delayed until denervation hypersensitivity has disappered. s) The distal sheath and all associated tissue is cleaned away. t) The basement membrane remains intact. Within the Perikaryon neurons u) Three to four nuclei occur v) No microtubules or microfilaments occur w) The axon hillock is rich in Nissil granules x) Nissil granules are ribosomes rich in RNA A nerve trunk is enclosed by y) Lamina densa and Schwann's sheaths z) Fine connective sheath called endoneurium aa) Dense collagenous fibers called perineurium ab) Loose collagenous and elastic fibers called epineurium ac) Interventricular foramen of Monro ad) Lateral foramen of Luschka ae) Cerebral aqueduct af) Arachnoid villi A motor unit constitutes ag) A single nerve cells (neuron) and all the muscle fibers it supplies ah) Multiple nerve cells (neurons) and all the muscle fibers they supply ai) A single nerve cell (neuron) and a single muscle aj) A nerve and muscle it supplies A myoneural (neuro-muscular) junction is ak) Refers to a motor end plate al) A point at which the nerve terminal and muscular tissue meet am) A point at which the nerve terminal and muscular tissue divide an) Characterized by contact between a nerve and a muscle fiber Nissil granules are: ao) Present in the cell body and in the axon ap) Easily stained in injured cells aq) Absent in the dendrites ar) Rich in RNA An example of pseudounipolar neuron is given by the as) Purkinje cells at) Pyramidal cells au) Spinal ganglion cells av) Cells of the posterior horn of the spinal cord Bipolar nerve cells are found in aw) The retina ax) The cochlea ay) The olfactory epithelium az) All of the above Schwann cells ba) Conduct impulses in peripheral nerves bb) Are embyonically derived from mesoderm bc) Myelinate axons of the peripheral nerves bd) Myelinate axons of the nerves in the CNS Which of the following occupies the free margin of the falx cerebri: be) Sigmoid sinus bf) Superior petrosal sinus bg) Superior sagittal sinus bh) Inferior sagittal sinus The central canal of the spinal cord is lined by: bi) Simple columnar epithelium bj) Simple squamous epithelium bk) Simple columnar ciliated epithelium bl) Pseudostratified columnar epithelium Myelin in the spinal cord is made by: bm) Microglia bn) Astrocytes bo) Schwann cells bp) Oligodendroglia The cerebellum has all of the following except bq) Purkinje cells br) Granular layer bs) Pyramidal cells bt) Molecular layer Which of the following is not a neurotransmitter bu) Heparin bv) Serotonin bw) Noradrenalin bx) Gamma aminobutyric acid (GABA) Organelles which are absent in neurons are: by) Centrioles bz) Lysosomes ca) Mitochondria cb) Golgi apparatus Telodendria cc) Lack microtubules cd) Are terminal branches of axons ce) Are terminal branches of dendrites cf) Are characteristics of oligodendrocytes The following are correct with regards to the third ventricle ECXEPT: cg) The lamina terminalis forms part of the wall that encloses the third ventricle. ch) The third ventricle opens caudally into the cerebral aqueduct. ci) The third ventricle lies within the Telecephalon. cj) The third ventricle communicates directly with the lateral ventricles by way of the interventricular foramen. Regarding synapses ck) Boutons always synapses with axons cl) Electrical synapses, also known as gap junctions are dominant between cardiocytes. cm) Chemical synapses usually function in the presence of a neurotransmitter cn) Axosomatic synapses are between axons and perikaryon Supporting cells in the central nervous system are generally named: co) Ganglion cp) Neuroglia cq) Schwann cells cr) Oligodendrocytes cs) Nuclei ct) Ganglia cu) Sciatic nerve cv) Brachial plexus cw) Unipolar cx) Bipolar cy) Multipolar cz) Pseudounipolar da) Ganglion db) Perikaryon dc) Astrocyte dd) Terminal bouton One of the below type of neuron is dominantly multipolar; de) Motor neurons df) Interneurons dg) Sensory neurons dh) Both a and b di) Myelinated nerve fibers dj) Cell bodies dk) Nodes of Ranvier dl) Neurofilaments dm) Pia mater dn) Dura mater do) Arachnoid dp) Both a and b dq) Tracts dr) Soma ds) Cortex dt) Nuclei du) Microglia dv) Astrocytes dw) Schwann cells dx) Ependymal cells The smooth surface that covers the brain but does not follow the underlying neural convolutions or sulci is the: dy) Pia mater dz) Dura mater ea) Neural cortex eb) Arachnoid membrane These are large ovoid sensory structures which serve as pressure receptors in the deep dermis: ec) Merkel Cells ed) Langerhans Cells ee) Paccinian Corpuscles ef) Meissner's Corpuscles Each of the following are differences in central nervous system versus peripheral nervous system myelin except: eg) Schmidt-Lanterman clefts exist in PNS myelin, but not in CNS myelin. eh) CNS myelin has no basal lamina but peripheral myelin does. ei) CNS proteins are glycoproteins while PNS proteins are glycolipids. ej) Oligodendroglia supply several internodal segments to CNS axons while Schwann cells supply a single internodal region. One of the following statements concerning the brain is correct:"},{"label":"A","text":"The forebrain may be subdivided into the cerebrum and the mesencephalon"},{"label":"B","text":"b) The brain is devided into hindbrain and forebrain"},{"label":"C","text":"c) The brainstem is a collective term for medulla oblongata, pons and midbrain"},{"label":"D","text":"d) The brain is surrounded with only 2 meninges: the pia mater and the arachnoid mater"},{"label":"A","text":"The white matter consists only of nerve fibres"},{"label":"B","text":"The gray matter consists of nerve cells embedded in neuroglia"},{"label":"C","text":"The nervous system is divided into 3 separate systems: the central nervous system, the peripheral nervous system and the autonomous nervous system"},{"label":"D","text":"The cavity of the hindbrain is the third ventricle The large anterior horn cells in the spinal cord are"},{"label":"A","text":"Inhibitory"},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha efferent"},{"label":"C","text":"Bipolar"},{"label":"D","text":"Upper motor neurons"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8636fc7df4ea","stem":"The anterior spinothalamic tract","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"crosses the midline of the midbrain"},{"label":"B","text":"crosses the midline of the medulla oblongata"},{"label":"C","text":"is uncrossed in the spinal cord"},{"label":"D","text":"crosses the midline of the spinal cord"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2020ffab3b91","stem":"The lateral corticospinal tract","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"crosses the midline in the pons"},{"label":"B","text":"crosses the midline in the medulla oblongata"},{"label":"C","text":"crosses the midline in the cervical spinal cord"},{"label":"D","text":"crosses the midline in the midbrain"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-91a9c0c12c8b","stem":"The corticospinal tract projects","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mainly to the putamen and caudatum"},{"label":"B","text":"Mainly to the pontile nuclei, which project to the basal ganglia"},{"label":"C","text":"Primarily onto interneurons in the spinal cord"},{"label":"D","text":"Primarily to the motoneurons in the spinal cord"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2f65d7c3e6c","stem":"The lateral spinothalamic tract terminates at","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve cells in the lateral gray horn of the spinal cord"},{"label":"B","text":"caudate nucleus"},{"label":"C","text":"Olivary nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"ventroposterolateral nucleus of the thalamus"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-86c9c3a68679","stem":"Concerning the caudate nucleus","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"there is a tail that ends at the septal nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"the main neurotransmitter is dopamine"},{"label":"C","text":"it is part of the neo-striatum joining with the putamen"},{"label":"D","text":"it is a nucleus in the lateral region of the thalamus"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2c9c200dbfd3","stem":"Concerning the cerebellum","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is located in the posterior cranial fossa beneath the tentorium cerebelli"},{"label":"B","text":"It contains the red nucleus"},{"label":"C","text":"There is a single dentate nucleus in the vermis"},{"label":"D","text":"There are connections with the spinal cord via the cerebello-spinal tract"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b1f593e6a085","stem":"Concerning the cyto-structure of a neuron","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"tubulin is heterodimer, consisting of one alpha and one beta subunit, in turn forming a protofilament, which in turn forms a microtubule of 25nm diameter"},{"label":"B","text":"microfilaments are two strands of actin that twist around each other"},{"label":"C","text":"all three fibres found in neurons are in equilibrium between the polymer and monomer form, but this may vary dependent upon concentrations"},{"label":"D","text":"all of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-80ea06c57c39","stem":"The myelin sheath","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is a collection of myelin molecules contained by the epineurium"},{"label":"B","text":"begins as an invagination of a single axon into the Schwann cell, which brings its outer cell membrane into close apposition and seals them together to form a sheet of internal membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"is made up of a mucin protein"},{"label":"D","text":"conducts electrical impulses"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ec2735876e6","stem":"Which of the following statements is TRUE concerning spina